WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.610
Darsi Tiny learned to ask

2
00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:04.190
before it sends a message.

3
00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:07.270
So, job done, the model is safe?

4
00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:10.100
Then someone typed: yes, send

5
00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:12.180
it, with nothing before it.

6
00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:14.720
It made up a message, and

7
00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:15.720
tried to send it!

8
00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:19.410
Today: guardrails, the safety

9
00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:20.730
rules you write in code.

10
00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:23.750
A model’s good habits are learned,

11
00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:25.890
and learned behaviour can fail.

12
00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:29.480
Darsi Tiny refuses the harmful

13
00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:30.980
requests it was trained on.

14
00:00:31.510 --> 00:00:33.190
But reworded in new ways,

15
00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:34.930
twenty-eight percent of the

16
00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:37.130
English ones got a tool call instead.

17
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:40.240
So real safety goes in your code.

18
00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:44.040
A guardrail is a rule in code that

19
00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:46.060
checks an action before it runs.

20
00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:49.060
It doesn’t guess, and it

21
00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:50.300
can’t be talked round.

22
00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:54.100
The model proposes; the guardrail decides:

23
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:57.760
run it, ask a person, or block it.

24
00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:01.090
Rule one: an allow-list.

25
00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:04.300
A weather helper needs the weather tool,

26
00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:06.700
not attendance and not messages.

27
00:01:07.510 --> 00:01:09.370
If the model asks for a tool

28
00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:10.950
off the list, block it.

29
00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:15.330
Rule two: actions wait for a person.

30
00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:17.480
The app shows the exact

31
00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:19.980
message and asks: yes or no?

32
00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:23.210
It remembers exactly what was approved.

33
00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:26.200
A yes to one message is

34
00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:27.620
not a yes to another.

35
00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:31.280
Rule three: check every

36
00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:32.920
detail before a tool runs.

37
00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:35.760
Is six B a class that exists?

38
00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:38.920
Is twenty-five o’clock a real time?

39
00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:41.380
Did every name come from the

40
00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:42.900
request or a result?

41
00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:47.710
Darsi Tiny once copied nine Z as nine C.

42
00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:49.360
Quick check!

43
00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:52.500
The person said yes to: the

44
00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:53.340
trip is cancelled.

45
00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:56.610
Now the model proposes a different

46
00:01:56.610 --> 00:01:59.030
message: pay the fees today.

47
00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:01.000
What should the guard return?

48
00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:03.040
Ask again.

49
00:02:03.730 --> 00:02:05.130
The yes was for a different

50
00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:06.530
message, so it doesn’t count.

51
00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:09.840
Compare the exact details, not just

52
00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:12.180
the word yes, and the model can’t

53
00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:13.420
send something nobody approved.

54
00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:17.210
Rule four: log everything.

55
00:02:17.990 --> 00:02:20.430
Every proposed action, what the guard

56
00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:22.690
decided, and who approved it.

57
00:02:23.270 --> 00:02:25.010
When something goes wrong, the

58
00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:26.270
log shows what happened.

59
00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:31.030
In the lab, choose what Darsi Tiny may do:

60
00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:34.350
read only, ask first, or allowed.

61
00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:36.550
Send a message to Mrs

62
00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:38.230
Mensah, and approve it.

63
00:02:38.710 --> 00:02:41.790
Then switch the code check off, send yes,

64
00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:45.490
send it, alone, and see what gets through.

65
00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:47.480
Your turn.

66
00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:51.680
Darsi Tiny learned to ask before sending.

67
00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:54.970
So where should the final check live?

68
00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:58.540
In the app’s code, with a person’s yes.

69
00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:01.350
Remember: allow only the

70
00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:02.630
tools a task needs.

71
00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:06.200
Run an action only after a yes

72
00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:08.000
to that exact action.

73
00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:11.710
Check every detail, and log everything.

74
00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:15.460
Try it yourself: on paper, write the

75
00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:17.640
guardrails for a homework-reminder agent.

76
00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:19.850
Which tools does it need?

77
00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:23.250
Which actions wait for a yes, and

78
00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:24.870
which details must you check?

79
00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:27.620
Then test them on a friend’s

80
00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:28.920
sneakiest requests.
